# .clinerules — model-agnostic rules for Cline
# Keep this file short: it is sent with every request and eats quota/context.

## 0. Language
- Talk to me in French. Write code, comments, commit messages and docs in English.

## 1. Project
- Goal: IOC Investigation Toolkit — a static Svelte (JavaScript) web app for cybersecurity analysts:
  a searchable catalog of online IoC pivot tools (src/data/tools.json) plus in-browser helpers
  (Fast analyze, Extract IoCs, Batch analysis, Email headers analysis).
- Stack: Svelte 5 + Vite (plain JavaScript with JSDoc types), no backend, deployed on GitHub Pages.
- Run: `npm run dev` (http://localhost:5173)
- Test: run all three after every change: `npm run check` (svelte-check), `npm run smoke`, `npm run theme`
- Build: `npm run build` (output in dist/)
- Key conventions:
  - Catalog: tools live only in `src/data/tools.json`. Follow the existing entry schema
    (id, name, url, categoryId, iocTypes, description, tags). Never hard-code tools in components.
  - Pure logic goes in `src/lib/utils/` (framework-agnostic, covered by `npm run smoke`).
    Services go in `src/lib/services/`. Components in `src/lib/components/` stay thin.
  - Dependencies are injected via the DI container (`src/lib/bootstrap.js`, `DI_TOKENS`).
    Do not import singletons or call `fetch`/`localStorage` directly inside components.
  - Styling: use only CSS tokens from `src/styles/theme.css`. Never hard-code a colour, radius,
    font size, shadow or spacing in a component (`npm run theme` will fail).
  - Privacy: everything runs in the browser. Never send pasted IoCs, headers or logs to any server
    other than the documented keyless public APIs, and only after an explicit user action.
  - Analysis output is factual: never derive a "Safe" / "Malicious" verdict. Show provider
    messages as-is and leave interpretation to the analyst.
  - Detected values are rendered as inert text. Never auto-open or auto-fetch a URL from user input.
- Out of scope: backend or database, accounts/login, API keys or paid providers, uploading files
  to third parties, automatic verdicts or scoring, e-commerce or ads.

## 2. Working method
- Before coding anything non-trivial, give a short plan (max 8 lines) and wait for my "ok".
- Work in small steps: one feature or fix per task. Never rewrite the whole project.
- If the request is ambiguous, ask ONE short question, then proceed with stated assumptions.
- After each step: tell me what changed, how to run/test it, and what the next step is.
- Prefer the simplest solution that works. No speculative features, no extra abstractions.

## 3. Token and request economy (free quotas are limited)
- Read only the files you need. Do not re-read a file you already have in context.
- Do not scan or list the whole repository unless I ask.
- Use targeted edits (diffs / replace), never re-output an entire file to change a few lines.
- Batch related edits into a single tool call when possible.
- Do not paste long code back to me in chat; the code goes in the files.
- If a task looks too big for one context window, split it and tell me.

## 4. Code quality
- Follow the conventions already present in the repo. Match existing style and naming.
- Keep functions small and named clearly. Comment the "why", not the "what".
- Validate inputs at boundaries. Handle errors explicitly; never swallow exceptions silently.
- Never hardcode secrets. Use environment variables and a `.env.example`; keep `.env` in `.gitignore`.
- Do not add a dependency without saying why. Prefer the standard library or what is already installed.
- Do not invent APIs or package names. If unsure a library/function exists, say so and check the docs or the installed package.

## 5. Testing and verification
- Add or update a test for each new behavior when a test setup exists (or propose a minimal one).
- Run the test command after changes and report the real result. Never claim "it works" without running it.
- When a command fails, read the actual error, fix the root cause, and retry at most 2 times.
  After that, stop and explain the situation instead of looping.

## 6. Git
- Use Git. Suggest a commit after each working step, with a clear message (imperative, < 72 chars).
- Never run destructive commands (`rm -rf`, `git reset --hard`, `git push --force`, dropping data) without my explicit approval.

## 7. Safety and boundaries
- Do not modify files outside the project folder.
- Do not touch `.git`, lockfiles, CI or config files unless the task requires it, and say so if you do.
- Ask before deleting files or running anything that installs global tools.

## 8. Documentation
- Keep `README.md` up to date: what it is, how to install, how to run, how to test.
- Keep a short `TODO.md` of remaining tasks so a new session can resume without re-explaining.
